<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imbros</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gökçeada</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Imbros (Turkish: Gökçeada) is an island in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a> near the entrance to the Dardanelles, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Held in antiquity mostly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and later by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, the Gattilusi lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and Ainos, and from the 15th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, it had a largely Greek population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> confirmed it as Turkish in 1923.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imbros.jpg" alt="Imbros" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SahinBasaran · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%B6k%C3%A7eada_Manzaras%C4%B1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
